# Contributing to <project> — the working protocol

> Drop this in your repo (adapted) when a second developer joins. It instantiates the
> [Cadmo collaboration protocol](https://github.com/tiagotorres91/cadmo/blob/main/docs/collaboration-protocol.md)
> — the full rationale and scars live there; this file is the operational instance.
> Maintainer, day 0: create the `absorbed`, `context-question` and `escalated` labels and grant the
> collaborator **triage or better** — rule zero depends on these facilities existing.

## Roles

- **Maintainer** (<@handle>): owns `main`, production, and content verification. Reviews everything, merges everything.
- **Collaborator** (<@handle>): picks up issues, builds on branches from up-to-date `main`, opens PRs — **and stops**. Never merges, never deploys.

## The rules

1. **One demand = one issue.** All conversation happens in the issue; the PR carries code + `Closes #N`, nothing else — except **one state-marker comment on the issue when the PR opens** ("PR #N open — with the maintainer"), so no later session re-picks the demand.
2. **The spec is in the issue body** (goal, acceptance criteria, size guidance). Read the WHOLE issue including comments before starting.
3. **Every maintainer review ends with a verdict:** ✅ APPROVED (merged, done) · 🔧 CHANGES (apply and resubmit) · ❓ DECIDE (answer and wait) · ❓ CONTEXT (a context-question answered — promoted to the context cache). A closed issue may instead carry **"🤖 Resolved by maintainer"** — not your work; just absorb the change.
4. **Merge is the full stop.** New topic → new issue. Two rounds on the same point → it escalates to the humans; the maintainer marks it `escalated` (rule zero skips it) and the human decision re-enters as a verdict that removes the label.
5. **Every branch starts from up-to-date `main`.** Never chain PRs.

## Rule zero — run this at the START of every session

Sweep three lists (repository state is the marker, never memory):

- **A — the ball is with you:** open issues with no comments, or where the maintainer commented last — **skipping issues assigned to someone else, and skipping `escalated`**. Before you start one: **assign yourself** (`gh issue edit <N> --add-assignee @me`) — the assignee is the claim.
- **B — outcomes to absorb:** closed issues without the `absorbed` label. Read the final verdict (the maintainer may have adjusted things in the merge), then add the label yourself:
  `gh issue edit <N> --add-label absorbed`
  (No triage permission? React 👍 on the closing comment — the maintainer mirrors reactions into the label on their next sweep.)
- **C — context questions:** open `context-question` issues that got an answer → incorporate it; your own unanswered blocking question → proceed under its documented **default assumption** (never deadlock waiting).

```bash
# List A — two native queries: unclaimed + claimed by you (skip escalated in both)
gh issue list --repo <owner>/<repo> --state open --search 'no:assignee -label:escalated' \
  --json number,title,comments \
  --jq '.[] | select((.comments|length)==0 or .comments[-1].author.login=="<maintainer>") | "#\(.number) \(.title)"'
gh issue list --repo <owner>/<repo> --state open --assignee "@me" --search '-label:escalated' \
  --json number,title,comments \
  --jq '.[] | select((.comments|length)==0 or .comments[-1].author.login=="<maintainer>") | "#\(.number) \(.title)"'
# List B
gh issue list --repo <owner>/<repo> --state closed --search '-label:absorbed' \
  --json number,title --jq '.[] | "#\(.number) \(.title)"'
# List C
gh issue list --repo <owner>/<repo> --state open --label context-question \
  --json number,title --jq '.[] | "#\(.number) \(.title)"'
```

## Context questions

Need maintainer context to proceed? Check `governance/context.md` (the answered-questions cache) first; still open, file a `context-question` issue with what's blocked and your **default assumption** (act under it if no answer comes — never deadlock). Answers get promoted to the cache: nobody asks twice.

## Building

Follow the project's `AGENTS.md` (the map + the definition of done). Highlights that bear repeating: acceptance criteria before code · test the real flow · say explicitly what you didn't test · a change touching a spec's watched files updates (or re-`--stamp`s) that spec in the same PR · production is never yours to touch.
